# Plan the next first-release unit

This is an authorized coordinator planning and queue-seeding unit. Do not implement product code in this card. The user has authorized planning the next first-release slice after the completed localization/F14 stop record.

## Required source and board inspection

Read `AGENTS.md`, `DECISIONS.md`, `tasks/queue.md`, `docs/fresh-start-reconciliation.md`, `docs/ai-run-state.md`, the relevant first-release task/evidence files, and the live board. Inspect the current repository refs and determine the authoritative accepted integration head; do not assume the F14 candidate `c2d0ef3...` was delivered. Current observed refs include `origin/main` at `e52efcded26d372aa7ed8751b347e288c8ef296f`, local recovery at `8239973844454a183b50ee8664db4d8451de5a51`, and the F14 accepted candidate was reviewed as based on `031a336...` but recorded as not delivered to main. Reconcile these facts before creating implementation cards.

## Planning objective

Select the smallest source-backed unit that advances the approved first release. The first-release authority in `docs/fresh-start-reconciliation.md` includes:

1. public/account foundation: public pages, login, logout, sessions, guest behavior, and verified PolarIS user lookup;
2. existing-user profile read using verified fields;
3. one website-owned content read;
4. one authorized website-owned write with same-transaction durable audit and injected audit failure;
5. staff enrollment and per-staff TOTP verification;
6. personal guestbook read with owner/friend privacy enforcement.

Respect all deferred gates: no production registration, payments, emulator writes, client handoff, staff operations, Pixel63, Atom, nginx/metrics/fuzzing, SWF, guessed schema, credentials, real user data, or unapproved production behavior. If the next first-release unit is not source-authorized, record the exact blocker instead of guessing.

## Jev requirement

After deterministic candidate reduction, use one batched, evidence-backed Jev judgment to rank/classify the candidate first-release units. Include `none of these` as a choice. Supply only facts already verified from the repository/board, record candidate count, batch size, Jev call count, confidence, selected unit, and source-verification result. Jev is advisory; Sol/coordinator verifies the result and retains schema, security, production, runner, and acceptance authority. If Jev is unavailable or disagrees with source, continue with Sol's documented decision or stop on ambiguity.

## Required queue result

Create a bounded graph for the selected unit:

- one implementation card per necessary profile (`backend`, `frontend`, `visual` only when exact isolated scope exists);
- one coordinator integration/acceptance card after implementation results;
- one independent `reviewer` card whose target candidate SHA is filled from the integration result before dispatch;
- one successor coordinator continuation card that will inspect the accepted result and plan the next unit.

Every card must include:

- exact allowed file paths;
- authoritative base SHA and ancestry requirement;
- accepted parent/dependency IDs and result requirements;
- evidence to produce;
- focused and full checks using the actual repository/toolchain;
- done criteria and stop conditions;
- profile owner, isolated worktree boundary, token/runtime cap;
- appropriate installed skills: `phpretro-roadmap-cards`, `phpretro-worker-handoff`, `phpretro-go-contracts`, `phpretro-react-boundary`, `phpretro-visual-fixtures`, `phpretro-independent-review`, or `phpretro-integration-acceptance` as applicable.

Do not create overlapping active edit scopes. Do not create cards for exact lookups or speculative schema/production work. If no unit can be authorized, create only a precise stop/blocker record and explain the missing authority.

## Dispatch and completion gate

Before unblocking or dispatching anything, verify the recorded base is an accepted ancestor of the current integration head, required parents are terminal and accepted, skills exist on the assigned profile, and no stale/worktree-only candidate is used. Then run a dispatcher pass and re-read the board. This coordinator card is not complete until either:

- the selected implementation/review/integration/continuation graph exists, dependencies are linked, at least one legitimate successor is ready/running, and the assigned workers are visibly dispatched; or
- an explicit source-backed stop reason is recorded, including why no first-release unit can be authorized.

Record the created card IDs, authoritative base SHA, exact dependencies, Jev evidence, dispatcher result, active/ready counts, remaining blockers, and the distinction between cards created, cards running, and cards merely waiting.

